# Desktop routing — SELKIES stack. Provisioned at build time into
# /etc/caddy/desktop.d/ and glob-imported by the main Caddyfile when
# DESKTOP_STACK includes selkies. Owns /desktop/ (runs parallel to /vnc/).
#
# Selkies is WS-ONLY on :6080 (does NOT serve the web client). Caddy serves the
# static client from /usr/share/selkies/web and reverse-proxies only the WebSocket.
# The client opens <path>/websockets (plural).
#
# AUTH MODEL — HONEST CAVEAT: Basic Auth gates the static client (the page load), but the
# WebSocket upgrade is NOT authenticated. Browsers do not attach basic-auth credentials to
# JS-initiated WebSocket handshakes (a 401 there → blank "Waiting for stream..."), and Selkies on
# :6080 has no auth of its own, so anyone who reaches :8080 can open /desktop/websockets and get
# the live desktop stream + input WITHOUT the password. Nothing here enforces same-origin (Caddy
# does not check Origin). This is tolerated ONLY because ingress is tunnel-only (no inbound ports,
# upstream bound to 127.0.0.1) and the DEFAULT stack is kasmvnc, which authenticates its WS
# natively end-to-end (see desktop-vnc.caddy). Running the selkies or "both" stack means treating
# the desktop as reachable by anyone who reaches the tunnel until Selkies-level auth is added.

# (1) WebSocket — must come BEFORE the static handler (longer path = higher priority).
@desktop_ws path /desktop/websockets
handle @desktop_ws {
	uri strip_prefix /desktop
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:6080
}

# (2) Static client — Caddy file_server serves the dashboard (Basic Auth gated).
handle_path /desktop/* {
	basic_auth {
		user {$DESKTOP_BCRYPT_HASH}
	}
	root * /usr/share/selkies/web
	file_server
}
handle /desktop {
	redir /desktop/ permanent
}
